It sure is annoying when you're hitting ESC to skip thousands of pline
messages and you end up dead with no way to see why.  So I wrote this
patch. Everything that would appear on the top line gets written to a
text file, "messagelog.txt", in the current directory. Menus, long
listings, help, etc. is not written.

I've only tested with win32 tty, but it's such a simple patch I don't
see why it wouldn't work in any environment that you can write to a
file.

Comments solicited.

--- nethack-3.4.3-orig\src\pline.c	2003-12-07 18:39:13.000000000 -0500
+++ nethack-3.4.3\src\pline.c	2006-08-16 15:10:09.968372500 -0400
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 static boolean no_repeat = FALSE;

 static char *FDECL(You_buf, (int));
+void FDECL(messagelog, (const char *));

 /*VARARGS1*/
 /* Note that these declarations rely on knowledge of the internals
@@ -66,6 +67,17 @@
 	if (vision_full_recalc) vision_recalc(0);
 	if (u.ux) flush_screen(1);		/* %% */
 	putstr(WIN_MESSAGE, 0, line);
+    messagelog(line);
+}
+
+void
+messagelog(line)
+const char *line;
+{
+    FILE *fp;
+    fp = fopen("messagelog.txt","a");
+    fprintf(fp,"%s\n",line);
+    fclose(fp);
 }

 /*VARARGS1*/


